ric, exasperating and absorbing as ever.</p><p>By â€œthe moviesâ€, incidentally, he means Hollywood, with a side order of established European and east Asian arthouse: he takes us from the cine-prehistory of photographer <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2010/sep/04/eadweard-muybridge-exhibition-rebecca-solnit">Eadweard Muybridge</a> to Fritz Langâ€™s Metropolis, DW Griffithâ€™s Birth of a Nation with its sinister Ku Klux Klan hero-worship, Chaplin, Stroheim, Hitchcock, Capra, Renoir, Bogart, Marilyn Monroe in The Seven-Year Itch, Monroe Stahr in The Last Tycoon, Scorseseâ€™s GoodFellas, David Lynchâ€™s Mulholland Drive (â€œIs it drive or dream?â€), Kirk&nbsp;Douglas and his alleged rape of Natalie Wood, Barbara Lodenâ€™s Wanda, Sean Bakerâ€™s Anora, Tracey Eminâ€™s Why I Never Became a Dancer and much more. He subtitles his book a â€œrevisionistâ€ history.</p> <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/books/2026/jul/20/a-sudden-flicker-of-light-by-david-thomson-review-a-brilliantly-eccentric-history-of-the-movies">Continue reading...</a>ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;otjUF9rFLXtuFoRt60nn8C1PkX1xqZzjW6taE1pWnF8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;m5CJLWsSMqkgKPqOWWNq6eFx5tWc2mv1lSbcbjetcpwƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ZiAJJMc0mPJSQt1S6dxihkC9DWpj8euXOPb666M6zBEƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;mTESxxNvcJLlIKl-mpfS3eYMQjMCZqO33eASAyCNZJQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;v3YeZO9xi76GtndAhbUDYIzRTZ71BJtsx5kXlFnoJc8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkdtrueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ylcuaDEjq7KQNXY9HWoj6_3oYTH3F57UE2hT8Y4SMtAƒdpairƒdleft